"People — DistriNet Research Unit (https://distrinet.cs.kuleuven.be/people/GintsEngelen)\nciteturn15search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; I joined DistriNet as a PhD Researcher in 2020. ... B-3001 Heverlee, BELGIUM ... Lisa Liu, Gints Engelen, Timothy Lynar, Daryl Essam, Wouter Joosen, Error Prevalence in NIDS datasets: A Case Study on CIC-IDS-2017 and CSE-CIC-IDS-2018, 2022 IEEE Conference on Communications and Network Security (CNS), 2022 IEEE Conference on Communications and Network Security (CNS), 10 pages, Austin, TX, USA, October 3 - December 5, 2022 ... Gints Engelen, Vera Rimmer, Wouter Joosen, Troubleshooting an Intrusion Detection Dataset: the CICIDS2017 Case Study, 6th International Workshop on Traffic Measurements for Cybersecurity, 2021 IEEE Security and Privacy Workshops (SPW), 6 pages, San Francisco, CA, USA, May 27, 2021\n\n# Gints Engelen\n\nImage: Gints Engelen\n\nI joined DistriNet as a PhD Researcher in 2020. Currently I am working in the domain of AI applications for Network Intrusion Detection Systems under the supervision of Prof. Lieven Desmet and Prof. Wouter Joosen.\n\nAs part of my previous work I have worked on improving Network Intrusion Detection datasets. My current research focuses on how Network Intrusion Detection Systems can be made robust in an evolving threat landscape, especially with the advent of new network protocols like QUIC.\n\n### Contact\n\nGints Engelen\n\nCelestijnenlaan 200A - bus 2402\nB-3001 Heverlee, BELGIUM\n\n Contact information\n\n \n\n## Recent publications\n\n  1. Robert Flood, Gints Engelen, David Aspinall, Lieven Desmet, Bad Design Smells in Benchmark NIDS Datasets, 9th IEEE European Symposium on Security and Privacy, 2024 IEEE 9th European Symposium on Security and Privacy (EuroS&P), pages 658-675, Vienna, Austria, July 8-12, 2024    \n  2. Lisa Liu, Gints Engelen, Timothy Lynar, Daryl Essam, Wouter Joosen, Error Prevalence in NIDS datasets: A Case Study on CIC-IDS-2017 and CSE-CIC-IDS-2018, 2022 IEEE Conference on Communications and Network Security (CNS), 2022 IEEE Conference on Communications and Network Security (CNS), 10 pages, Austin, TX, USA, October 3 - December 5, 2022   \n  3. Gints Engelen, Vera Rimmer, Wouter Joosen, Troubleshooting an Intrusion Detection Dataset: the CICIDS2017 Case Study, 6th International Workshop on Traffic Measurements for Cybersecurity, 2021 IEEE Security and Privacy Workshops (SPW), 6 pages, San Francisco, CA, USA, May 27, 2021    --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMachine Learning in Network Intrusion Detection: A Cross-Dataset Generalization Study (https://iris.unicas.it/retrieve/a40146bd-61d7-4a77-98c7-cf72f480c839/2024%20-%20Machine%20Learning%20in%20Network%20Intrusion%20Detection%20-%20A%20Cross-Dataset%20Generalization%20Study.pdf)\nciteturn15search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.0 years ago; Crawled: 2.0 years ago; Digital Object Identifier 10.1109/ACCESS.2024.3472907 ... Corresponding author: Marco Cantone (marco.cantone@unicas.it)\nReceived 21 August 2024, accepted 24 September 2024, date of publication 3 October 2024, date of current version 14 October 2024.\n\nDigital Object Identifier 10.1109/ACCESS.2024.3472907\n\nAPPLIED RESEARCH\n\n# Machine Learning in Network Intrusion Detection: A Cross-Dataset Generalization Study\n\nMARCO CANTONE, CLAUDIO MARROCCO (Member, IEEE), AND ALESSANDRO BRIA  \nDepartment of Electrical and Information Engineering, University of Cassino and Southern Latium, 03043 Cassino, Italy  \nCorresponding author: Marco Cantone (marco.cantone@unicas.it)\n\nThis work was supported in part by Italian Ministry of University, Ministry of Education, University and Research (MIUR) Program “Department of Excellence” Law under Grant 232/216; and in part by the “Innovative Ph.D. Programs for Public Administration under Grant D.M.351/2022.”\n\n**ABSTRACT** Network Intrusion Detection Systems (NIDS) are a fundamental tool in cybersecurity. Their ability to generalize across diverse networks is a critical factor in their effectiveness and a prerequisite for real-world applications. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nError Prevalence in NIDS datasets | Tools and Documentation (https://intrusion-detection.distrinet-research.be/CNS2022/Tools_Documentation.html)\nciteturn15search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4.0 years ago; We also include on this page high-level documentation for the datasets that generalises across different attack classes. ... All code that we used for our paper can be found in this repository. ... Our fixed version of the CICFlowMeter tool can be found at https://github.com/GintsEngelen/CICFlowMeter.\n# Tools & Documentation\n\nOn this page we gather all the tools and code that we wrote / adapted during the course of our work on this paper. We also include on this page high-level documentation for the datasets that generalises across different attack classes.\n\n## Paper Code repository\n\nAll code that we used for our paper can be found in this repository. This includes labelling code for the datasets as well as code used for our experiments.\n\n## Improved CICFlowMeter Tool\n\nOur fixed version of the CICFlowMeter tool can be found at https://github.com/GintsEngelen/CICFlowMeter.\n\n#### Changes made in previous work\n\n  * A TCP flow is no longer terminated after a single FIN packet. It now terminates after mutual exchange of FIN packets, which is more in line with the TCP specification.\n  * An RST packet is no longer ignored. Instead, the RST packet also terminates a TCP flow\n  * CICFlowMeter fixed as per this GitHub pull request (Fwd and Bwd Bulk features affected)\n\n#### Changes made during this work\n\n  * Changed the timestamp to output in UTC and with microsecond precision\n  * Added ICMP protocol support as well as a bunch of ICMP-related features\n  * Added Total TCP Duration feature\n  * Added Forward RST and Backward RST features\n  * Fixed TCP Segmentation Offset issue (more details in our paper under section IV-B.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nError Prevalence in NIDS datasets | Datasets Download (https://intrusion-detection.distrinet-research.be/CNS2022/Dataset_Download.html)\nciteturn15search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4.0 years ago; # Datasets Download ... As discussed in our paper, we opted to use the \"Attempted\" label for an attack (e.g.\n# Datasets Download\n\n### BEFORE USING THE DATASETS:\n\nAs discussed in our paper, we opted to use the \"Attempted\" label for an attack (e.g. DoS GoldenEye - Attempted) whenever a flow that was intended to be part of the attack does not exhibit any malicious behaviour. Researchers are free to decide for themselves whether they want to combine the \"Attempted\" flows into the actual attack label, as this will depend heavily on the flow preprocessing method that is applied. When in doubt, we recommend to relabel all \"Attempted\" flows as Benign (the easiest way to do this is to look at all flows where the \"Attempted Category\" feature is different from -1). Under no circumstance should the \"Attempted\" flows be treated as a separate label for your Machine Learning model!\n\nThe datasets can be downloaded here--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEnsemble Learning for Network Intrusion Detection Based on Correlation and Embedded Feature Selection Techniques (https://www.mdpi.com/2073-431X/14/3/82/xml)\nciteturn15search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.6 years ago; Crawled: today; MDPI and ACS Style ... Computers 2025, 14, 82. https://doi.org/10.3390/computers14030082 ... Nassreddine, Ghalia, Mohamad Nassereddine, and Obada Al-Khatib.\n## Share and Cite\n\nMDPI and ACS Style\n\nNassreddine, G.; Nassereddine, M.; Al-Khatib, O. Ensemble Learning for Network Intrusion Detection Based on Correlation and Embedded Feature Selection Techniques. Computers 2025, 14, 82.